#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "3b9c93fe-82ac-58c5-b14a-9bcd30b1f0e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3175" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the denominator using\n\n$$\n1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x} &= \\frac{\\cos x \\cdot 1}{\\sin x \\cdot \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\cot x\\csc x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "e5821060-9f69-5df5-9456-a4574e92e910" :question/math-academy-id "e-3176" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using\n\n$$\n1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 - \\sin x} &= ((1 - \\sin x)(1 + \\sin x))/(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= ((1 - \\sin x)(1 + \\sin x))/(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + \\sin x}{1} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "056204bc-53b9-5a32-a53c-0f728729fd9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3332" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nto further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\cos^{2} x\\sec^{2} x - 3\\cos^{2} x &= 3\\cos^{2} x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cos x})^{2} - 3\\cos^{2} x \\\\\n&= 3\\cos^{2} x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x} - 3\\cos^{2} x \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\cos^{2} x}{\\cos^{2} x} - 3\\cos^{2} x \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\cos^{2} x}{\\cos^{2} x} - 3\\cos^{2} x \\\\\n&= 3 - 3\\cos^{2} x \\\\\n&= 3(1 - \\cos^{2} x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nto rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901fb9c5-25ff-5cfd-ad3a-5e4dd673bbb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058610} {:answer/id #uuid "938f75f5-7827-5d56-8bcf-6ba9760488c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058611} {:answer/id #uuid "edd3e6f8-5a29-545d-ab4d-ed9f68fab862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058612} {:answer/id #uuid "501d4a84-03d5-5012-8a5c-cdfa69819cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058613} {:answer/id #uuid "79d9be8d-49f3-571c-9564-1890c8c04089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79d9be8d-49f3-571c-9564-1890c8c04089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058614} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbdca89-59b7-52fd-85f3-bf7334d417d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058609}] :question/id #uuid "6649e183-eb4d-5947-a240-b0aee73aa4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-65182" :question/problem "If $\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x} = (1)/(f(x))$, then $f(x) =$"}]}
